\section{Starting values}{

\code{start_pars} takes any of a model's estimated parameters, one, several or
all of them. \code{beta} can be a named subset of the coefficients, or unnamed
with one value per column. \code{sigma2_re} can be a named subset of the
variances, or unnamed with one value per term in the order of the formula.
Every value must be finite, and every one but the coefficients positive.
The others take their automatic start, given as \code{start} by
\code{\link[=glgpm_parameters]{glgpm_parameters()}}. \code{regression} is the coefficient of the regression
without the latent effects, \code{family_regression} the size of the negative
binomial regression without them, \code{one} the value 1 and
\code{distance_quantile} the 10th percentile of the distances between the
locations. A name the model lacks stops with the names it has.
}

\section{Held values}{

\code{fixed_pars} of \code{\link[=glgpm]{glgpm()}} takes the same names and structure, and holds
the parameters it gives at their values instead of estimating them. It
holds the variance of a nugget (\code{tau2}, for a model with
\code{gp(nugget = TRUE)}), the measurement error variance of a Gaussian model
(\code{sigma2_me}, which may be zero) and the size of a negative binomial model
(\code{theta}). \code{\link[=coef.RiskMap]{coef.RiskMap()}} gives the held parameters with the estimated
ones, so \code{start_pars = coef(fit)} works for a refit with the same holds,
and \code{\link[=summary.RiskMap]{summary.RiskMap()}} shows them as fixed.
}

